Proposition 7.10 states that the power domain \(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{D}\) is a neighbourhood system whenever \(\mathcal{D}\) is, and is effectively given whenever \(\mathcal{D}\) is: \(\mathcal{D}^\dagger=\{\downarrow X\mid X\in\mathcal{D}\}\) with \(\downarrow X=\{Y\in\mathcal{D}\mid Y\subseteq X\}\) is positive, the closure of a positive system under finite unions, including the empty union, is a neighbourhood system because intersection distributes over union, and omitting the empty union would leave a positive system; the neighbourhoods \(\bigcup_{i<q}\downarrow X_{n_i}\) are indexed by finite sequences through a recursive coding, an intersection is the double union \(\bigcup_{i,j}\downarrow(X_{n_i}\cap X_{m_j})\) after the empty terms are deleted by finitely many decidable tests, each remaining intersection is replaced by an index \(k_{ij}\), and the double index is flattened; equality of neighbourhoods reduces to \(\downarrow X_k\subseteq\bigcup_i\downarrow X_{n_i}\), equivalently \(\exists i.\,X_k\subseteq X_{n_i}\), which is decidable from \(\mathcal{D}\). `PowerDomain` is that neighbourhood system, with master \(\downarrow\Delta\) and with intersection closed by `PDmem_inter`. The intersection argument is the distribution Scott uses: `PDmem_biUnion_inter` and `PDmem_upSet_inter_biUnion` induct on the list, and `PDmem_upSet_inter` rewrites \(\downarrow X\cap\downarrow Y\) as \(\downarrow(X\cap Y)\) when the intersection is a neighbourhood and as empty otherwise. `Ypd` sends a code to the union of the down-sets of its decoded list, with code \(0\) the empty union (`Ypd_zero`) and the successor of a pair a cons cell (`Ypd_cons_code`); `Ypd_isPDmem` and `PDmem_exists_Ypd` show that this enumerates the power-domain neighbourhoods. `interCode` folds `outerInterStp` along the first list and `innerInterStp` along the second: `Ypd_innerstep` prepends the intersection index when the consistency decider returns \(1\) and leaves the accumulator unchanged when it returns \(0\), in which case `upSet_inter_eq_empty_of_not_cons` makes the down-set intersection empty, and `Ypd_interCode` concludes that the coded neighbourhood equals the intersection. `upSet_subset_Ypd_iff` is the equivalence \(\downarrow X_a\subseteq Y_k\leftrightarrow\exists b.\,X_a\subseteq X_b\), `Ypd_subset_iff` applies it to every generator, and `Ypd_eq_iff` is both directions; `subCode_computable` decides that bounded quantification from the inclusion of \(\mathcal{D}\), and `eqCode_computable` conjoins the decider with its swap. `Ypd_interEq_computable` is Definition 7.1(i), the three-place test that the intersection of two coded neighbourhoods equals a third. `PDPresentation` takes the master index to be the code of the singleton list containing the original master, and takes consistency to hold of every code because the empty union is contained in every neighbourhood. `PowerDomain_isEffectivelyGiven`, named `theorem_710`, builds that presentation from the consistency decider of \(\mathcal{D}\). The gloss of `eqCode_computable` calls two-place equality relation (i); relation (i) is the three-place intersection test, and two-place equality is the mutual-inclusion argument at the end of Scott's proof. The general lemma for an arbitrary positive system, the parenthetical that deleting the empty union leaves a positive system, and the remark that finite unions of a general effectively given system need not be effectively given are not separate theorems, and the intersection code has one entry per consistent pair rather than a padded length \(q\cdot r\). **Theorem:** yes — `PowerDomain` is the neighbourhood system of finite unions of down-sets, and `theorem_710` is effectivity whenever \(\mathcal{D}\) is effectively given. **Proof:** yes — distribution of intersection, deletion of inconsistent pairs, choice of intersection indices, and the bounded inclusion test are the steps given for this power domain.
